lag_of_Spain.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail) [Spain](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spain_national_football_team \"Spain national football team\") (2nd title)\n\nRunners-up | ![](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Flag_of_Argentina.svg/40px-Flag_of_Argentina.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail) [Argentina](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argentina_national_football_team \"Argentina national football team\")\n\nThird place | ![](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/b/be/Flag_of_England.svg/40px-Flag_of_England.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail) [England](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/England_national_football_team \"England national football team\")\n\nFourth place | ![](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c3/Flag_of_France.svg/40px-Flag_of_France.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail) [France](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/France_national_football_team \"France national football team\")\n\nTournament statistics\n\nMatches played | 104\n\nGoals scored | 308 (2.96 per match)\n\nAttendance | 6,810,966 (65,490 per match)\n\nTop scorer | [![France national football team](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c3/Flag_of_France.svg/40px-Flag_of_France.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/France_national_football_team \"France national football team\") [Kylian Mbappé](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kylian_Mbappé \"Kylian Mbappé\") (10 goals)\n\nBest player | [![Spain national football team](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/9a/Flag_of_Spain.svg/40px-Flag_of_Spain.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spain_national_football_team \"Spain national football team\") [Rodri](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rodri_\\(footballer,_born_1996\\) \"Rodri (footballer, born 1996)\")\n\nBest young player | [![Spain national football team](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/9a/Flag_of_Spain.svg/40px-Flag_of_Spain.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spain_national_football_team \"Spain national football team\") [Pau Cubarsí](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pau_Cubarsí \"Pau Cubarsí\")\n\nBest [goalkeeper](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goalkeeper_\\(association_football\\) \"Goalkeeper (association football)\") | [![Spain national football team](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/9a/Flag_of_Spain.svg/40px-Flag_of_Spain.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spain_national_football_team \"Spain national football team\") [Unai Simón](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unai_Simón \"Unai Simón\")\n\nFair play award | ![](//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Flag_of_the_Netherlands.svg/40px-Flag_of_the_Netherlands.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail) [Netherlands](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netherlands_national_football_team \"Netherlands national football team\")\n\n← [2022](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_FIFA_World_Cup \"2022 FIFA World Cup\") *[2030](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2030_FIFA_World_Cup \"2030 FIFA World Cup\")* →\n\nInternational football competition\n\nThe **2026 FIFA World Cup**[\\[A\\]](#cite_note-3) was the 23rd [FIFA World Cup](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FIFA_World_Cup \"FIFA World Cup\"), the [quadrennial](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quadrennial \"Quadrennial\") international men's [soccer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soccer \"Soccer\") championship contested by [the national teams](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_men's_national_association_football_teams \"List of men's national association football teams\") of the member associations of [FIFA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FIFA \"FIFA\"). The tournament began on June 11, 2026, and concluded on July 19 with [Spain](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spain_national_football_team \"Spain national football team\") winning the championship for the second time.[\\[3\\]](#cite_note-Schedule-4) It was jointly hosted by 16 cities—11 in the United States, 3 in Mexico, and 2 in Canada. The tournament was the first FIFA World Cup to be hosted by three countries and the first to include 48 teams, an expansion from the previous 32-team format.[\\[4\\]](#cite_note-5)\n\nThe 2026 tournament was the second World Cup to be hosted by multiple nations, after the [2002](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2002_FIFA_World_Cup \"2002 FIFA World Cup\") edition. Mexico became the first country to host the World Cup three times, having hosted the [1970](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1970_FIFA_World_Cup \"1970 FIFA World Cup\") and [1986](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1986_FIFA_World_Cup \"1986 FIFA World Cup\") tournaments.[\\[5\\]](#cite_note-qt-6) The United States previously hosted the [World Cup in 1994](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1994_FIFA_World_Cup \"1994 FIFA World Cup\"), while it was Canada's first time hosting the men's tournament.[\\[5\\]](#cite_note-qt-6) The event returned to its traditional June–July schedule after the [2022 World Cup](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_FIFA_World_Cup \"2022 FIFA World Cup\") in [Qatar](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qatar \"Qatar\"), which was uniquely held in November and December to avoid [Qatar's extreme summer heat](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Climate_of_Qatar \"Climate of Qatar\").[\\[6\\]](#cite_note-7) [Canada](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canada_men's_national_soccer_team \"Canada men's national soccer team\"), [Mexico](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico_national_football_team \"Mexico national football team\"), and the [United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_men's_national_soccer_team \"United States men's national soccer team\") qualified automatically as hosts, while [Cape Verde](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cape_Verde_national_football_team \"Cape Verde national football team\"), [Curaçao](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curaçao_national_football_team \"Curaçao national football team\"), [Jordan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jordan_national_football_team \"Jordan national football team\"), and [Uzbekistan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uzbekistan_national_football_team \"Uzbekistan national football team\") made their World Cup debuts.[\\[5\\]](#cite_note-qt-6)\n\nFor the first time in the tournament's history, the top four teams from the [FIFA Men's World Ranking](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FIFA_Men's_World_Ranking \"FIFA Men's World Ranking\") at the time of the contest ([Argentina](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argentina_national_football_team \"Argentina national football team\"), [Spain](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spain_national_football_team \"Spain national football team\"), [France](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/France_national_football_team \"France national football team\"), and [England](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/England_national_football_team \"England national football team\")) advanced to the semifinals.[\\[7\\]](#cite_note-8) Spain won [the final](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_FIFA_World_Cup_final \"2026 FIFA World Cup final\") against defending champion Argentina 1–0 after [extra time](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extra_time \"Extra time\"), winning its second title and first since [2010](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2010_FIFA_World_Cup \"2010 FIFA World Cup\"). Spain became the first nation to become the reigning champion for both the men's and [women](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2023_FIFA_Women's_World_Cup \"2023 FIFA Women's World Cup\")'s World Cups simultaneously,[\\[8\\]](#cite_note-9) and the first team to win the World Cup while conceding only one goal during the entire tournament. France captain [Kylian Mbappé](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kylian_Mbappé \"Kylian Mbappé\") won his second consecutive [Golden Boot](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FIFA_World_Cup_Golden_Boot \"FIFA World Cup Golden Boot\") for scoring the most goals (10) during the tournament. He became the [all-time leading goalscorer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_FIFA_World_Cup_top_goalscorers \"List of FIFA World Cup top goalscorers\") at the World Cup and the first player to win multiple World Cup Golden Boots.[\\[9\\]](#cite_note-10) The average number of goals per match in the tournament was 2\\.96, the highest since the 1970 edition.[\\[10\\]](#cite_note-11)\n\nSpain's captain [Rodri](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rodri_\\(footballer,_born_1996\\) \"Rodri (footballer, born 1996)\") won the [Golden Ball](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_Ball_\\(FIFA_World_Cup\\) \"Golden Ball (FIFA World Cup)\") as the best player of the tournament. [Unai Simón](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unai_Simón \"Unai Simón\") won the [Golden Glove](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FIFA_World_Cup_Golden_Glove \"FIFA World Cup Golden Glove\") as the tournament's best [goalkeeper](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goalkeeper_\\(association_football\\) \"Goalkeeper (association football)\"), while [Pau Cubarsí](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pau_Cubarsí \"Pau Cubarsí\") won the [Young Player Award](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FIFA_World_Cup_Young_Player_Award \"FIFA World Cup Young Player Award\") as the tournament's best young player. Nearly 7 million people attended matches during the tournament, surpassing the 1994 edition to set a new record for the [highest attendance in World Cup history](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FIFA_World_Cup_records_and_statistics#Attendance \"FIFA World Cup records and statistics\").[\\[11\\]](#cite_note-USATodayRecord2026-12) The 2026 World Cup reportedly generated a record $15 billion in revenue.[\\[12\\]](#cite_note-13)\n\n## Format\n\n### Expansion\n\nThe idea of expanding the tournament had been suggested as early as 2013 by then-[UEFA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UEFA \"UEFA\") president [Michel Platini](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michel_Platini \"Michel Platini\"),[\\[13\\]](#cite_note-14)[\\[14\\]](#cite_note-15) as well as in 2016 by current FIFA president [Gianni Infantino](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gianni_Infantino \"Gianni Infantino\").[\\[15\\]](#cite_note-16) Opponents of the proposal argued that the number of matches played was already at an unacceptable level, that the expansion would dilute the quality of the matches,[\\[16\\]](#cite_note-17)[\\[17\\]](#cite_note-18) and that the decision was driven by political rather than sporting concerns, specifically that Infantino was trying to [win his election](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2016_FIFA_Extraordinary_Congress \"2016 FIFA Extraordinary Congress\") by promising to bring more countries to the World Cup.[\\[18\\]](#cite_note-19)\n\nStarting with the 2026 edition, the World Cup expanded to 48 teams, an increase of 16 teams compared to the previous seven tournaments.[\\[19\\]](#cite_note-48teams-20) As approved by the [FIFA Council](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FIFA_Council \"FIFA Council\") on March 14, 2023, the teams were split into 12 groups of 4 teams, with the top 2 teams in each group and the 8 best third-place teams progressing to a new round of 32\\.[\\[20\\]](#cite_note-IMC-21) It was the first expansion and format change since [1998](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FIFA_World_Cup_1998 \"FIFA World Cup 1998\").[\\[21\\]](#cite_note-22)\n\nThe total number of matches played increased from 64 to 104, and the number of matches played by teams reaching the semifinals increased from 7 to 8\\. The tournament lasted 39 days, an increase from the 32 days of the [2014](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2014_FIFA_World_Cup \"2014 FIFA World Cup\") and [2018](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2018_FIFA_World_Cup \"2018 FIFA World Cup\") tournaments, and from the 29 days of the [2022](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_FIFA_World_Cup \"2022 FIFA World Cup\") tournament,[\\[22\\]](#cite_note-23)[\\[23\\]](#cite_note-24) although each team still played three group matches.[\\[24\\]](#cite_note-25)[\\[25\\]](#cite_note-26) The final matchday at club level for players named in the final squads was May 24, 2026; clubs had to release their players by May 25, with exceptions granted to players participating in continental club competition finals up until May 30\\. The combined rest, release, and tournament periods remained 56 days, the same as in the [2010](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2010_FIFA_World_Cup \"2010 FIFA World Cup\"), 2014, and 2018 World Cups.[\\[20\\]](#cite_note-IMC-21)\n\n#### Other expansion formats explored\n\nAfter the expansion to 48 teams was approved on January 10, 2017, it was initially decided that the tournament would include 16 groups of 3 teams, with the top two teams of each group progressing to a round of 32\\.[\\[19\\]](#cite_note-48teams-20)[\\[26\\]](#cite_note-27) This format would have included 80 matches in total, with the maximum number of matches per team remaining at seven, although each team would have played one fewer group match than in the 2022 tournament. The tournament would still have been completed within 32 days.[\\[27\\]](#cite_note-28) This format was chosen over three alternatives involving 40 to 48 teams, 76 to 88 matches, and a minimum of 1 to 4 matches per team.[\\[B\\]](#cite_note-32)\n\nCritics of this format argued that the use of three-team groups with two teams progressing significantly increased the risk of collusion between teams.[\\[31\\]](#cite_note-guyon-33) This prompted FIFA to suggest that [penalty shootouts](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penalty_shoot-out_\\(association_football\\) \"Penalty shoot-out (association football)\") could be used to prevent draws in the group stage,[\\[32\\]](#cite_note-34) although even then some risk of collusion would remain, as it was possible for the two teams playing the last group match to engineer a [circle of death](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Circle_of_Death_\\(sporting\\) \"Circle of Death (sporting)\") result that would allow both of them to qualify.[\\[31\\]](#cite_note-guyon-33) The tournament format was finalized in 2023, with an announcement that there would be 12 groups of 4 teams.[\\[33\\]](#cite_note-35)[\\[34\\]](#cite_note-36)\n\n### New rules\n\nThe 2026 FIFA World Cup introduced several [rule](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laws_of_the_Game_\\(association_football\\) \"Laws of the Game (association football)\") changes, primarily designed to reduce [time-wasting](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Running_out_the_clock \"Running out the clock\").[\\[35\\]](#cite_note-newrules-37) The new rules included:\n\n- 10-second substitutions: Players being [substituted](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Substitute_\\(association_football\\) \"Substitute (association football)\") had ten seconds to exit the field; otherwise, the substitute had to wait for one minute before entering the match.\n\n- 5-second restarts: A visual 5-second countdown was shown by the referee for throw-ins and goal kicks in situations of time-wasting. If the ball was not put into play in time, a restart was awarded to the opposing team; a [corner kick](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corner_kick \"Corner kick\") was awarded if the infraction occurred with a goal kick.\n\n- Medical treatment: Any outfield player who received medical attention on the field had to leave the field and wait for 1 minute before returning to play.\n\n- Expanded [video assistant referee](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Video_assistant_referee \"Video assistant referee\") (VAR): The VAR could review and overturn wrongly awarded corner kicks, certain attacking fouls, and clear mistakes on red cards given from a second yellow card.\n\n- Mouth-covering red cards: To stop confrontational or insulting behavior which was concealed to avoid [lip-reading](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lip-reading \"Lip-reading\"), any player who covered his mouth with his hand, arm, or shirt while confronting an opponent was [sent off](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_card_\\(soccer\\) \"Red card (soccer)\").\n\n- Player leaving the field in protest: Players or officials who left the field in protest were sent off.[\\[35\\]](#cite_note-newrules-37)\n\nAnother change was the tiebreaker formula used when two or more teams finish the group stage with an equal number of points. The first tiebreaker criteria was the number of points earned in head-to-head matches between the tied teams, not the [goal difference](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goal_difference \"Goal difference\") among all matches.[\\[C\\]](#cite_note-41)\n\n### Mandatory breaks\n\nCooling or [hydration breaks](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydration_break \"Hydration break\") were introduced at the [2014 World Cup](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2014_World_Cup \"2014 World Cup\") in Brazil. For the 2026 World Cup, FIFA introduced mandatory three-minute hydration [breaks in every half for all matches](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time-out_\\(sport\\) \"Time-out (sport)\"). Broadcasters were permitted to run [commercials](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Television_timeout \"Television timeout\") during these pauses.[\\[39\\]](#cite_note-Breaks-42) Since the clock did not stop during the breaks, the three minutes were automatically included in the [stoppage time](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoppage_time \"Stoppage time\") for each half.[\\[40\\]](#cite_note-Yahoo-43)\n\n### Match schedule\n\nThe match schedule, without group assignments, was announced on February 4, 2024\\.[\\[D\\]](#cite_note-46) On June 13, FIFA released an updated schedule, with specific pairings assigned to venues for the knockout stage.[\\[43\\]](#cite_note-47) The full schedule was unveiled in a live broadcast on December 6, 2025, the day after the [draw](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_FIFA_World_Cup_draw \"2026 FIFA World Cup draw\").[\\[44\\]](#cite_note-48) The opening match of the tournament included Mexico, and took place on June 11, 2026, at the [Estadio Azteca](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Estadio_Azteca \"Estadio Azteca\") in [Mexico City](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico_City \"Mexico City\").[\\[45\\]](#cite_note-49) Canada's first match occurred on June 12 at [BMO Field](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BMO_Field \"BMO Field\") in [Toronto](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toronto \"Toronto\"), while the United States' opening match took place on the same day at [SoFi Stadium](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SoFi_Stadium \"SoFi Stadium\") in [Inglewood](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inglewood,_California \"Inglewood, California\"). The national team of each host nation played its three group stage matches in its own country.[\\[41\\]](#cite_note-ny-44)\n"
